 "genre_note": "Cluster c15 is Sporting News output and splits into three genres that decompose very differently.\n\n(1) Nine live game blogs (7 NFL primetime, 1 college football, 2 MLS) make up ~24,000 of the 40,700 words. Each is one document but four distinct subjects: an editorial recap, a reverse-chronological play-by-play, a pregame block of inactives/injuries/storylines, and a boilerplate \"what channel / start time\" block. Word count never sets the boundary here; the play-by-play is split at the halftime marker, which is the real sub-topic seam, and that split happens to land each half in the 600-1,600 word band. The how-to-watch boilerplate is near-verbatim across documents, so it is factored into three shared broadcast notes (SNF/NBC + Tirico-Collinsworth-Stark from doc_0143/0200/0275; MNF/Buck-Aikman from doc_0004/0290; TNF/Amazon from doc_0035) rather than duplicated seven times.\n\n(2) Four sportsbook affiliate guides (doc_0062, 0207, 0264, 0368) are the trap case: each is a single URL but ten-plus unrelated items (operator profiles, sign-up steps, bet-type glossaries, a PGA Tour schedule, a legality history). They are decomposed by item, and the recurring operators (DraftKings, FanDuel, BetMGM, BetRivers) get one shared note each drawing on doc_0264 and doc_0368. Two documents (doc_0062, doc_0207) bundle all four operators inside a single un-splittable block, so those blocks form one comparative-rankings note instead \u2014 block granularity, not preference, forced that. The four near-identical account sign-up passages are likewise merged into one procedure note so the evidence is not split four ways.\n\n(3) Five feature/preview pieces (doc_0105 Argentina-Paraguay, doc_0167 England-South Africa, doc_0231/0249 USMNT, doc_0203 Inter Miami tickets, doc_0273 Showtime Boxing, doc_0328 Harper controversy) are the most conventional and yield clean entity notes.\n\nBuilding-block distribution is as the brief predicts: empirical_observation dominates (~70 of 113 notes), concept next (~33), with procedure appearing only in the betting guides (4). Genuinely rare blocks that DO occur and were not manufactured: two `model` notes (early-vs-late futures betting as an information/odds trade-off, doc_0062; why NBA lines move more than other sports, doc_0368), two `argument` notes (the Springboks prediction in doc_0167 and the Musah-at-No.6 verdict in doc_0249), and three `counter_argument` notes clustered in the media-ethics story (Rizzo's attack on Mintz, the BBWAA rebuttal, plus the Chelsea Janes defence classed as `argument`). `hypothesis` does not occur anywhere in these 20 documents \u2014 that is a finding, not a gap, and none was invented.\n\nTwo dropped-block checks worth recording. doc_0088[104] reads like a routine \"MORE:\" cross-link but actually carries the substance of Messi's injury (\"fatigue\" and a \"scar tissue issue\"), so it was assigned to the Messi note rather than dropped with the other promos. doc_0225[60] looks like a throwaway pregame line but names the UCLA homecoming throwback uniforms, so it was assigned. Conversely doc_0143's repeated \"TV: #MIAvsPHI on NBC / Stream on #NFLPlus\" tweet footers and doc_0275's equivalents are pure promotion inside embedded tweets and were dropped.\n\nCoverage: 18 of 20 documents land at 94-98% of words assigned. Two land lower and both are structurally, not carelessly, thin: doc_0105 (89%) is a short preview whose non-content is section headers plus four affiliate promos (\"LIVE BLOG:\", \"MORE: Watch Premier League with Fubo\", betting-odds referral); doc_0231 (93%) is similar. Nothing substantive was discarded in either.\n\nCross-document entity sharing is concentrated in the soccer material and is where multi-hop evidence should live: Lionel Messi's injury/return spans doc_0041, doc_0088 and doc_0105; Inter Miami's playoff elimination spans doc_0041 and doc_0088; the 2024 ticket-price backlash spans doc_0203 and doc_0088 (the supporters' protest at DRV PNK is the same story as the Miami Herald price release); Gio Reyna and Tyler Adams each span doc_0231 and doc_0249. No (doc, block) pair is assigned twice anywhere in the plan."
